## 9. Risks & Open Questions

| Risk / Question | Why it matters |
|---|---|
| Sending raw journal text to Gemini's API | Sensitive personal data leaving the system boundary — needs a documented data-handling and consent policy. |
| No crisis-detection flow specified | A mental-wellness app that doesn't handle self-harm signals is a real safety gap, not just a feature gap. |
| AI output reliability (malformed JSON, hallucinated emotion labels) | `/api/analyze/{id}/` needs defined fallback behavior if Gemini's response can't be parsed. |
| Cost scaling | Gemini API calls per entry + per weekly summary will scale with active users; cost model isn't addressed in the spec. |
| No age restriction defined | Should explicitly state a minimum age / adult-only audience given the sensitive subject matter. |

---

## 10. Out of Scope (v1)

- Therapist/clinician-facing features or multi-party accounts.
- Real-time chat or conversational AI companion.
- Mobile native apps.
- Predictive ML mood forecasting.
- Community/social features.
- Curated mental-health resource library.

---

## 11. Conclusion

MindTrack AI's MVP is narrow and well-defined: register, write, get AI-analyzed emotion + mood score + a suggestion. The five MVP features (auth, journal CRUD, emotion detection, mood scoring, wellness suggestion) are sufficient to ship a coherent first version. Sentiment analysis, weekly summaries, and trend analytics are the natural next layer once the MVP loop is validated. Mobile, voice, ML prediction, and community features are appropriately deferred. The main gap to close before launch — beyond the original spec — is a clear policy on data privacy/consent and a basic safeguard for crisis-related content, given the sensitivity of the domain.